Allergic Rhinitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Clear watery rhinorrhea, Paroxysmal sneezing, Pale boggy nasal turbinates

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-004
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting paroxysmal sneezing and perceived respiratory distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe paroxysmal respiratory presentation with bilateral nasal congestion leading to obligate mouth breathing and acute panic. An urgent physical assessment was required to differentiate severe allergic rhinitis from acute asthma exacerbation or early anaphylaxis, ensuring airway stability and providing immediate pharmacological relief before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I cannot stop sneezing, my face is itching uncontrollably, and I can barely catch my breath through my mouth."
History of Present Illness: Acute severe paroxysmal episodes of repetitive sneezing commencing 4 hours ago after cleaning a dusty attic. Accompanied by copious, clear, watery rhinorrhea and intense pruritus of the nose, eyes, and soft palate. Bilateral nasal congestion has caused obligate mouth breathing, which triggered significant anxiety and a sensation of dyspnea. Notes tearing and ocular pruritus. No history of previous anaphylaxis.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently incapacitated by sneezing fits and anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme distress and sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Agitated, holding a box of tissues, exhibiting frequent paroxysmal sneezing and the "allergic salute" (upward rubbing of the nose).
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals pale, bluish, boggy, and swollen nasal turbinates. Copious clear, watery secretions present. Oropharynx shows a cobblestone appearance of the posterior wall due to lymphoid hyperplasia. Bilateral conjunctival injection and excessive tearing noted. Allergic shiners (darkened infraorbital venous pooling) present. Chest auscultation is completely clear with equal air entry bilaterally, no wheeze, stridor, or prolonged expiratory phase.
Environmental Observations: High dust environment, multiple pets indoors.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Acute Allergic Rhinitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of systemic signs such as hypotension, generalized urticaria, angioedema, or wheeze.

Acute Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear chest auscultation, normal SpO2, and absence of lower airway wheezing or coughing.

Viral Upper Respiratory Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sudden onset immediately following massive allergen exposure, extreme pruritus, and lack of fever or purulent discharge.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with extreme distress secondary to a massive acute aeroallergen exposure, mimicking respiratory distress due to obligate mouth breathing and panic. Urgent face-to-face assessment was entirely necessary to definitively rule out lower airway compromise (asthma) or evolving anaphylaxis. Clinical examination confirmed the pathology was strictly localized to the upper respiratory tract. Immediate administration of systemic antihistamines and topical steroids was required to break the paroxysmal cycle, alleviate the patient's acute distress, and prevent an unnecessary emergency department presentation overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Cetirizine 10mg STAT. Prescribed Intranasal Fluticasone Propionate 2 sprays per nostril daily. Prescribed Olopatadine 0.1% eye drops BD.
Positive Behavioral Change: To minimize allergen load, institute a strict routine of showering and washing hair immediately after heavy dust or pollen exposure. This provides immediate reduction in facial/ocular pruritus (positive reinforcement), embedding the behavior of proactive allergen decontamination.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): High-efficiency particulate air (HEPA) filter recommended for the bedroom.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks for consideration of radioallergosorbent testing (RAST).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if patient develops swelling of the lips/tongue/throat, widespread hives, difficulty breathing, wheezing, or a sensation of impending doom.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical and isolated to allergic etiology; does not preclude future asthmatic responses.
Clinical Course: Antihistamines will provide relief within 60 minutes. Nasal steroids require days of consistent use to achieve maximal mucosal shrinking.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your breathing. If you start wheezing, feeling tight in the chest, or your throat feels swollen, this is a severe allergic reaction requiring an ambulance (000).
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The oral antihistamine blocks the immediate itch and sneeze reflex. The nasal steroid reduces the internal boggy swelling to allow you to breathe through your nose again.
Actionable Steps: Avoid the trigger environment (attic) until professionally cleaned. Shower immediately to wash off trapped allergens. Use the nasal spray aiming away from the nasal septum.

